<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Why does backward Euler slightly <em>under</em>-shoot the RLC peak with a coarse step?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>Backward Euler adds numerical damping: for a pure oscillator its amplification factor per step has magnitude below 1, so energy is lost artificially. Smaller steps (or trapezoidal integration, which preserves oscillation amplitude) reduce it.</p>
</div></details></div>
